  "op": {
   "title": "Organophosphate poisoning",
   "path": [
    "Pharmacology",
    "Autonomic Nervous System",
    "Cholinesterase inhibitors"
   ],
   "en": "Organophosphates, like the insecticides malathion and parathion, bind permanently (irreversibly) to acetylcholinesterase (AChE). AChE's job is to break down acetylcholine. With AChE blocked, acetylcholine piles up everywhere it is released, causing a long-lasting cholinergic overload. Treatment has three parts. First, decontaminate to reduce absorption. Second, give atropine, the cornerstone of treatment, which blocks acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors. Third, give an oxime such as pralidoxime (2-PAM), which can reactivate AChE, but only if it is given before the enzyme ‘ages’ and the bond becomes permanent.",
   "am": "Organophosphates (ለምሳሌ malathion እና parathion የተባሉ ፀረ-ተባይ መድሃኒቶች) acetylcholinesterase (AChE) ከሚባለው ኢንዛይም ጋር በቋሚነት (irreversibly) ይጣበቃሉ። የ AChE ሥራ acetylcholine (ACh)ን ማፍረስ ነው። AChE ሲታገድ ACh በሚለቀቅበት ቦታ ሁሉ ይከማቻል፤ ውጤቱም ለረጅም ጊዜ የሚቆይ ከመጠን ያለፈ cholinergic ተጽእኖ ነው። ሕክምናው ሦስት ክፍሎች አሉት፦ 1) Decontamination — ተጨማሪ መምጠጥን (absorption) መቀነስ። 2) Atropine — የሕክምናው ዋና መሰረት (cornerstone)፤ muscarinic receptors ላይ ACh የሚያደርገውን ተጽእኖ ያግዳል። 3) Pralidoxime (2-PAM) — AChEን መልሶ ሊያነቃ ይችላል፣ ነገር ግን ኢንዛይሙ 'aging' ከማድረጉ (ትስስሩ ቋሚ ከመሆኑ) በፊት ቶሎ መሰጠት አለበት።",
